\enlargethispage{12\baselineskip}
\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}
\vspace{-4pt}

\paragraph{Scope and limitations.}\looseness=-1
All findings are correlational (confounded by data distribution and optimization; $N = 2$ VLM-to-VLA transitions; Appendix~\ref{sec:claim_evidence_appendix}); we analyze causal-decoder VLAs on LIBERO only (flow-matching~\cite{black2024_pi0} and diffusion~\cite{ghosh2024_octo} architectures may differ); all analyses are open-loop.
Trained $\Delta R^2$ correlates with published success rates ($\rho = 0.583$, $p = 0.10$; Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_discussion_appendix}) but is underpowered ($n = 9$; effect size is large, $|\rho| > 0.5$); tracing collapse onset remains for future work.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}\looseness=-1
Our findings yield three actionable diagnostics for VLA development.
%
\emph{(1)~Checkpoint selection.}
$\Delta R^2$ provides a cheap probe-based signal ($> 0.10$ flags reorganization) requiring only a forward pass and a linear probe, replacing costly rollout evaluation whose wall-clock cost scales with environment complexity (Appendix~\ref{sec:extended_discussion_appendix}).
Practitioners can rank checkpoints by $\Delta R^2$ and reserve rollout budgets for the top candidates.
%
\emph{(2)~Transfer learning strategy.}
The flat VLA-to-VLA $\Delta R^2$ profile (\S\ref{sec:r2_collapse}) shows that VLA-to-VLA transitions preserve exit structure, justifying staged pipelines (VLM$\to$VLA$\to$task) that reuse the first-stage action head without re-learning exit representations; inherited L1 routing can further serve as a frozen prior, reducing tunable parameters during domain transfer.
%
\emph{(3)~Mid-layer probe value.}
Where exit collapse is pronounced, mid-layer representations ($R^2$ peak at L9--L11 across architectures) outperform exit-layer features for downstream tasks such as reward modeling, distillation targets, or auxiliary prediction heads; the distributed instruction integration pattern (\S\ref{sec:routing_synthesis}) additionally implies that uniform layer pruning is preferable to attention-score-based head selection when compressing VLAs.
%
Together, the entry-exit decomposition provides architectural guidance that complements existing VLA training recipes.
Code and data will be released upon acceptance.

